{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And YHWH relented that He had made man on the earth.</b> The word עשה (made) signifies the joining of form to substrate (golem), for the צלם (image) is the form and the דמות (likeness) is the substrate; therefore it says, 'בצלם אלהים ברא אותו — in the image of Elohim He created him.' And in this verse, in the account of the generations of Adam, it says, 'בדמות אלהים עשה אותו — in the likeness of Elohim He made him.' It mentioned the term בריאה (creation) in connection with the form, and mentioned עשייה (making) in connection with the likeness. And it says, 'בדמות אלהים עשה אותו,' meaning He joined the form to the substrate. And since the meaning of דמות is substrate, when it says 'בדמות אלהים' it is like 'עבד אלהים — a servant of Elohim,' for the servant is outside of Him. Therefore He relented over this joining, to say that it would have been better had the form remained in its place and not been joined with this substrate, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'It would have been better for man had he not been created.' And this is why it says 'כי עשה את האדם בארץ — that He had made man in the earth' — it did not say 'על הארץ — upon the earth,' because they were submerged in physical desires.'" "<b>Now reflect.</b> The intent of Solomon, peace be upon him, when he praised the form that was never joined to the substrate at all, is what he said (Ecclesiastes 4:3): 'ושבח אני את המתים — and I praised the dead,' and so on, 'וטוב משניהם את אשר עדן לא היה — and better than both is he who has not yet been.' And the investigative scholars have already likened these three ways to a parable. The first — the form that was never joined to the substrate at all — they likened to a king sitting in his palace. The second — the form that separated from the substrate — they likened to a king who emerged from prison and returned to his kingdom. The third — the form still joined to the substrate — they likened to a king imprisoned in prison. And there is no doubt that the king's rank is far greater in the first than in the second, and in the second greater than in the third. And it is fitting to ask here: if so, that the first is more excellent, why was it joined, and why does Scripture praise this joining at the conclusion of the work of Creation, saying, 'וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה והנה טוב מאד — and Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good'? And this praise, which praised the totality, would include as well the joining of the form to the substrate in man created on the sixth day; if so, how did Scripture praise the joining of the form to the substrate and include it in 'והנה טוב מאד,' and Solomon, peace be upon him, comes to praise it when not joined to the substrate, contrary to the words of Moses? And he said: 'וטוב משניהם את אשר עדן לא היה.' And the answer to the matter is that even though the first is more excellent than the two from the perspective that it did not suffer pain or harm and was not attached to the substrate, nevertheless once it is attached to the substrate and then separates from it while still pure as when it came, then its excellence becomes public and it is proven like gold. Therefore the souls of the righteous yearn to return to their first place, and for this reason Moses, peace be upon him, praised this joining, for it is praiseworthy when it returns pure as when it came, since it succeeded in its mission. And the plain sense of the verse is: האדם בארץ — 'the man who is on the earth.' For the relenting was because of man's sin. Similarly (Isaiah 62:7): 'עד יכונן ועד ישים את ירושלים תהלה בארץ,' its meaning being: until He sets praise upon the Jerusalem that is on the earth. And likewise it is written explicitly (Psalms 122:2–3): 'ירושלים הבנויה כעיר שחוברה לה יחדו — Jerusalem built up, a city joined together' — Jerusalem built below as a city joined together above." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'וינחם ה׳ — and YHWH relented.' [This is] the Name that is first among the thirteen attributes. 'כי עשה את האדם בארץ — that He had made man on the earth' — on account of the earth it is that He relented, for it is the cause." "<b>And it grieved Him to His heart.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the word לב (heart) in reference to the Holy One, blessed be He, is nothing but a figure of speech, and the same applies to the other physical organs in man that are attributed to Him, blessed be He, such as 'פי ה׳ — the mouth of HaShem,' 'עיני ה׳ — the eyes of HaShem,' 'אזני ה׳ — the ears of HaShem,' 'יד ה׳ — the hand of HaShem,' 'רגלי ה׳ — the feet of HaShem.' But since it has already been established among us that the Holy One, blessed be He, desires the good, as it is written (Isaiah 42:21): 'ה׳ חפץ למען צדקו — HaShem desired for the sake of His righteousness,' and does not desire evil, as it is written (Psalms 5:5): 'כי לא אל חפץ רשע אתה — for You are not an El who desires wickedness,' therefore, when Scripture wishes to inform us that when what the Holy One, blessed be He, desires is done, it says of Him that He rejoices, as it is written (Psalms 104:31): 'ישמח ה׳ במעשיו — HaShem shall rejoice in His works,' and when what He does not desire is done, it says of Him, 'ויתעצב אל לבו — and it grieved Him to His heart.' For it is impossible for us to apprehend His will to perform His commandment and to guard against transgressions unless He makes it known to us through these attributes, which are attributes of human beings. Therefore, when He, blessed be He, reflects within Himself, from the place of His thought, Scripture mentions in Him 'His heart,' for the heart in man is the instrument of the thought that comes to it from the brain. And so you will see that one who wishes to seclude himself and to draw down the inflow of thought bends his head and thinks, so that the inflow of thought descends from the brain — which is the source of thought — to the heart. And even though this is a figure of speech and far removed from the thing signified, nevertheless the thing signified is [grasped] in its imagery, and from this the thought is so called, for its inflow and power descends from above — to his heart. And likewise the emissary sent from before Him, blessed be He, to speak a word in His name, is called 'פי ה׳ — the mouth of HaShem,' as it is written (Isaiah 34:16): 'כי פי הוא צוה — for His mouth has commanded,' that is, my angel; 'ורוחו הוא קבצן — and His wind gathers them,' meaning, the wind (spirit) of the angel called פי (mouth). And so Yonatan translated: 'ומלאכיה — and His angels.' Also the prophets are called 'פי ה׳.' Also the Urim and Tummim. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Joshua 9:14): 'ואת פי ה׳ לא שאלו — and they did not ask the mouth of HaShem' — these are the Urim and Tummim. And when [the emissary] is sent to oversee the deeds of creatures, to punish or reward them, he is called 'עין — an eye,' as it is written (Psalms 34:16): 'עיני ה׳ אל צדיקים — the eyes of HaShem are toward the righteous,' (II Chronicles 16:9): 'ה׳ עיניו משוטטות — HaShem's eyes range,' (Proverbs 15:3): 'בכל מקום עיני ה׳ צופות — in every place the eyes of HaShem watch,' (Zechariah 3:9): 'על אבן אחת שבעה עינים — upon one stone seven eyes,' meaning many providences. And when the emissary is sent to hear a matter or to receive it, he is called 'אזנים — ears,' as it is written (Numbers 11:1): 'ויהי העם כמתאוננים רע באזני ה׳ — and the people were as complainers, evil in the ears of HaShem.' For just as the ear in man is the instrument of hearing, so the angel sent is an instrument to hear that matter, to bring it and receive it, and he is called by the name of the instrument of hearing in man. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Psalms 40:7): 'אזנים כרית לי — ears You have dug for me' — 'the ears above are not open except to hear the prayers of Israel.' This comes to explain that the designated angel who waits until the last congregation among Israel finishes praying — he and his forces are called by the name 'ears,' to hear the prayers and take them all and make a crown and set it on the head of the King where He is. And when [the emissary] is sent to do battle with a nation, to strike them or uproot them, he is called 'יד ה׳ — the hand of HaShem,' as it is written concerning the Philistines (I Samuel 5:11): 'כבדה מאד יד האלהים שם — the hand of Elohim was very heavy there,' and concerning Egypt (Exodus 9:3): 'הנה יד ה׳ הויה — behold, the hand of HaShem will be,' and at their drowning: 'את היד הגדולה — the great hand.' And when His emissaries and His retinue gather to effect a new matter in the world, they are called 'רגליו — His feet,' as it is written regarding the future (Zechariah 14:4): 'ועמדו רגליו ביום ההוא על הר הזיתים — His feet shall stand on that day on the Mount of Olives,' which means His hosts. And so you will find that Scripture said regarding the hosts of Israel (Numbers 20:19): 'ברגלי אעבורה — I will pass through on foot,' whose meaning is 'בצבאותי — with my hosts,' as I shall mention there, with the help of HaShem." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'ויתעצב אל לבו — and it grieved Him to His heart.' Rabbi Ami said: A parable — to a king who built a palace through an architect, and it did not please him. On whom to vent his anger? On the architect! Another parable: A king who made merchandise through a broker and did not succeed in it — on whom to vent his anger? On the broker! Understand this forceful parable. And since in the parable of the architect there was room to err in two authorities, for a person might say: even though the king is king, the architect is wiser than he in that craft — and the matter is not so, heaven forbid, for all is one unified whole without division — therefore Rabbi Ami went back and mentioned the parable of the broker, for the broker has no power at all over the merchandise and has no share in it whatsoever. And the intent of Rabbi Ami in both parables is that the architect is the broker and the broker is the architect. The meaning of אדריכל (architect): craftsman or builder. The Targum [renders] (I Kings 5:32): 'ויפסלו בוני שלמה — and Solomon's builders hewed,' [as] 'ובנו אדריכלי שלמה — and Solomon's architects built.'" "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah.</b> 'And it grieved Him to His heart' — this is the architect and the intermediary (סרסור) mentioned in the Midrash, and there they explained the secret of the matter: 'And the King — YHWH — will speak with him,' and it says, 'I will blot out the man whom I have created…' 'for I repent' — כי עשיתם is the writing (כתיב). And you should rouse yourself to understand and to gain insight into the expression 'man upon the earth,' which Scripture mentions twice in two verses one after the other: for in the first it speaks below, and in the second above. And the word ויתעצב returns to 'the man' — he is Tiferet Yisrael, on whose account YHWH made 'the earth' in the verse of Genesis, which caused all the aforementioned evil. And the meaning of the verse in its secret will be וינחם: the intention is that He made 'the man,' and the consolation (נחמה) was not for the sake of the man but for the earth, that is, on account of the earth that was the cause; therefore ויתעצב האדם אל לבו — 'the man grieved to His heart' — on account of 'his heart,' which was the cause, for he is the earth and the intermediary and the architect. And since it is written at the beginning of creation, 'Let YHWH rejoice in His works,' He now mentions concerning him ויתעצב אל לבו. And let it not be difficult for you that He used an expression of sadness (עצבון) in this place, and likewise what is written (Isaiah 22:12): 'And Adonai YHWH of hosts called on that day for weeping,' etc. — for they already said in the Gemara: this is in Battei Gavai, this is in Battei Barai, and from Gevurah downward is called Battei Barai — and understand this. And just as we find at the time of anger before the flood ויתעצב אל לבו, so too Scripture mentions at the time of favor after the flood and the acceptance of the offering: ויאמר ה' אל לבו לא אוסיף עוד, and this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "The citation in the source is given as ישעיהו ס״ב:ז׳ but the phrase cited matches Isaiah 62:7; normalized to standard verse numbering.", 2 "The phrase 'השם הראשון שבי״ג מדות' appears to identify the divine Name YHWH (ה׳) as the first of the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy; rendered literally without expansion.", 5 "The phrase כי עשיתם כתיב indicates a ketiv/qre variant or textual note; rendered literally. The Aramaic(ized) terms 'Battei Gavai' and 'Battei Barai' are transliterated without resolution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:28.923840169Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}